/* THE 2025 CLOSE, IMPORTED FROM THE FILE THAT WAS ALREADY EVIDENCE.
   Owner instruction, 29 August 2026.

   WHAT WAS WRONG. InventoryPointInTime-MC281714-2025-12-31.xls has sat in
   docs/metrc-exports since 10 Aug, hashed, registered in source_export and
   marked used_in_audit = true — while metrc_rpt_point_in_time held ZERO rows
   for 2025-12-31. The file was being treated as evidence by the register and
   was not in the database at all. Loading a table is not delivering the data,
   and registering a file is not loading it either.

   3,364 ROWS, AND THE FILE SAYS SO ITSELF. Its title block reads "Total
   Records: 3,364"; the sheet parses to 3,364 data rows below the header; every
   Tag Number is distinct. Three counts, one number. The assertion at the foot
   of this migration re-derives it after the insert and RAISES if it is not
   exactly 3,364, so a partial load rolls back rather than standing as a close.

   NO QUANTITY IS WRITTEN, BECAUSE THERE IS NONE. The Metrc Inventory Point in
   Time report has no weight, count or unit-of-measure column — verified against
   this file's own 13 columns and against every source_row already stored. This
   import records WHICH TAGS were held on 31 December 2025 and nothing about how
   much. Any pound figure for that close remains a reconstruction from other
   sources and must keep saying so.

   THE EMPTY CELL IS STORED AS THE EMPTY STRING, not as null, because that is
   what the three existing imports did. One convention, not two.

   THE PRIMARY KEY CANNOT HOLD TWO LICENCES AND THIS DATE DOES NOT NEED IT TO.
   metrc_rpt_point_in_time is keyed (as_of_date, tag) with no licence, which
   silently drops a tag held under both licences at one instant — it has already
   done so on 2026-08-06, and the damage there is worse than a drop (see the
   companion finding). 2025-12-31 is unaffected: only the MC281714 file exists
   for that date and no row exists at it yet. This migration therefore does NOT
   change the key. That is a separate, reviewed change, not something to smuggle
   in behind an import.

   IDEMPOTENT AND SELF-CANCELLING. It refuses to run twice: if any row already
   exists at 2025-12-31 it raises rather than upserting, because an upsert is
   exactly what produced the 2026-08-06 hybrids.

   TO REVERSE: delete from metrc_rpt_point_in_time where import_id =
   '4c1d9f6a-2e30-4b77-9c85-7f0a1d3e2b41'.
*/

do $$
declare
  k_import  constant uuid := '4c1d9f6a-2e30-4b77-9c85-7f0a1d3e2b41';
  k_as_of   constant date := date '2025-12-31';
  k_licence constant text := 'MC281714';
  k_expect  constant integer := 3364;
  v_before  integer;
  v_after   integer;
  v_tags    integer;
begin
  select count(*) into v_before from metrc_rpt_point_in_time where as_of_date = k_as_of;
  if v_before <> 0 then
    raise exception 'metrc_rpt_point_in_time already holds % row(s) at %. This import refuses to upsert: overwriting a point-in-time row is what produced the 2026-08-06 hybrids.', v_before, k_as_of;
  end if;

  insert into metrc_rpt_point_in_time (
    as_of_date, tag, record_type, name, category, strain, location, sublocation,
    status_current, expiration_date, sell_by_date, use_by_date, licence,
    source_row, import_id, imported_at)
  select
    k_as_of,
    btrim(r->>'Tag Number'),
    nullif(r->>'Type', ''),
    r->>'Name',
    r->>'Category',
    r->>'Strain',
    r->>'Location',
    r->>'Sublocation',
    r->>'Status Current',
    nullif(r->>'Expiration Date', '')::date,
    nullif(r->>'Sell By Date', '')::date,
    nullif(r->>'Use By Date', '')::date,
    k_licence,
    r,
    k_import,
    now()
  from jsonb_array_elements($payload$
